 { Mircea Parligras – Ian Nepomniachtchi, Aix-les-Bains 2011 Learning objective: This game provides an example for an ideal execution of the e4-e5 advance, as well as the subsequent kingside attack. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. e4 Nxc3 6. bxc3 Bg7 7. Be3 c5 8. Qd2 cxd4 9. cxd4 Nc6 10. Rd1 O-O 11. Nf3 Bg4 12. Be2 Rc8 13. O-O { So far we have been following a well-known theoretical line, and now Black decided to deviate with: } Bxf3 ( { More common is } 13... Qa5 { when pretty much all GM-level games have finished in a draw, so it is understandable for Nepomniachtchi to have chosen differently, although his strategy ultimately backfired... } ) 
14. Bxf3 Na5 { Aiming to play ...Nc4xe3. } ( { An interesting option was } 14... e5 $5 15. d5 Nd4 16. Rc1 $1 { preparing Be2 with a small advantage } ( { but not } 15... Na5 15. Be2 $4 Rc2 $19 ) 
Nxf3 17. gxf3 $14 { when White’s king is reasonably safe, while the d5-pawn is strong. } ) 
15. Bg5 $1 { Suddenly Black’s knight will not have much to do on c4. } ( { Probably Nepomniachtchi was hoping for } 15. Be2 Nc4 16. Bxc4 Rxc4 17. Rc1 b5 { when Black had reasonable chances to play for a win, as in the game Praszak – Eljanov, Polanica Zdroj 1999. } ) 
b6 16. e5 $1 { A good positional decision. Generally speaking, the e4-e5 advance is double-edged in the Grünfeld structure. On the positive side, it restricts the action of the g7-bishop and creates some attacking chances on the kingside. On the negative side, it may permanently weaken the d5 square, and it often gives up the possibility of creating a passed d-pawn, as Black can simply neutralize the majority with the standard ...e7-e6. This position is different, and the key is the lack of Black’s light-squared bishop and the misplacement of Black’s knight, many moves away from the d5-square. } Qd7 ( { The imprecise } 16... Nc4 $6 { is met by } 17. Qb4 $1 Rc7 ( { or } 17... Re8 18. Bb7 $1 Rc7 19. Ba6 Na5 20. Bb5 Rf8 21. Rc1 $16 ) 
18. Be2 Na5 19. Rc1 $16 { and the e7-pawn is a serious target. } ) 
17. Rfe1 ( { PH: Nowadays we would just play } 17. h4 { and get the attack going, AlphaZero style. } ) 
Nc4 18. Qe2 e6 { A necessary move. It weakens the f6-square somewhat, but White was threatening 19. Bg4 e6 20. d5!. } ( { For example, the careless } 18... b5 $2 { loses to: } 19. Bg4 $1 e6 20. d5 Qe8 21. dxe6 fxe6 22. Rd6 $1 { This is the key. } Nxd6 23. exd6 Kh8 24. Bxe6 ) 
19. h4 { A familiar plan, intending to create more weaknesses around Black’s king. } h6 $6 { This weakens the position needlessly. } ( { Krasenkow suggests the variation } 19... b5 $5 20. h5 Nb6 21. h6 Bh8 22. Qd2 Rc4 23. Qf4 Nd5 24. Bxd5 exd5 25. Bf6 $6 Qe6 { when Black is holding okay, but instead } 25. Re3 $5 { ⩲ allows White to maintain some pressure. PH: I think Flores Rios understates White's advantage. } 25. a3 a5 26. Rd3 b4 27. Rf3 Qe6 28. Rc1 { when modern engines say +2. The bishop on h8 is simply too boxed in. } ) 
20. Bc1 b5 ( { If } 20... h5 $2 21. g4 $1 { Black is in serious trouble after } hxg4 22. Be4 $1 { when the simple Qxg4 followed by h4-h5 blows up Black’s castled position: } Rfd8 23. Qxg4 Qb5 ( { or } 23... Kf8 24. h5 gxh5 25. Qxh5 $18 ) 
24. Bg5 Rd7 25. h5 { With a winning attack. } ) 
21. h5 g5 22. Be4 { White’s position is very promising. He may proceed with either f2-f4 opening up the kingside, or Bb1 planning Qc2-h7#. } Nb6 $6 { This makes things clearer for White. } ( { If } 22... Rfd8 23. f4 gxf4 24. Bxf4 $16 { and Black’s position is quite fragile. } ) 
 ( { A better defence was } 22... a6 $5 23. Bb1 ( { another option is } 22... Nb6 22. Qg4 $5 { preparing f2-f4 } ) 
f5 24. exf6 Rxf6 25. a4 $14 { with a generally pleasant position for White. } ) 
23. Bd3 { With the double threat Bxb5 and Qe4-h7#, when there is nothing better than giving up a pawn with: } Nd5 ( { If } 23... b4 $6 { White gains a very powerful attack after: } 24. Qe4 f5 ( { or } 24... Rfd8 25. Bxg5 $1 hxg5 26. h6 Bxh6 27. Qh7 Kf8 28. Qxh6 Ke8 29. Qxg5 $16 ) 
25. exf6 Rxf6 26. Qh7 Kf8 27. d5 $1 { +- Opening up lines with a winning position, for example: } Nxd5 28. Bb2 Rf7 ( { or } 28... Nc3 29. Ba6 $1 { +- } ) 
29. Bxg7 Rxg7 30. Qxh6 $18 ) 
24. Bxb5 ( 24. Qe4 Rfd8 25. Qh7 Kf8 26. Be4 $16 ) 
Qe7 25. Bd2 { White is simply a pawn up, and the rest of the game is not so relevant to us, thus I leave it with only sparse comments. } f5 26. exf6 Rxf6 27. Bc4 Qf7 28. Bxd5 $5 { Keeping things simple and clear, even at the cost of decreasing the advantage somewhat. } ( { An option was } 28. Bd3 $1 { with a near-decisive advantage. } ) 
exd5 29. Bb4 Rf4 30. Bc5 Bf8 $2 ( { A better defence was: } 30... Re4 $1 { ± } ) 
31. Qe6 ( { More potent was } 31. Qa6 $1 Rc7 32. Bxf8 Qxf8 33. Qg6 Rg7 34. Qc6 { winning another pawn, and the game. } ) 
Qxe6 32. Rxe6 Kf7 33. Ra6 Bxc5 34. dxc5 Rxc5 35. Rxh6 Ra4 36. Rb1 $18 { The invasion on the seventh rank decides the game. } d4 37. Rb7+ Kg8 38. Rg6+ Kh8 39. Rh6+ Kg8 40. Rd6 Rc8 41. h6 d3 42. g3 ( { But not: } 42. Rg7 Kh8 43. Rdd7 $2 Rc1 44. Kh2 Rh4 45. Kg3 Rxh6 $16 ) 
 ( { The text move makes a space on g2 for the king, and covers the h4-square, which forced Black to resign. For example, } 42. g3 Rxa2 $4 43. Rg7 Kh8 ( 43. g3 Kf8 44. Rf6 Ke8 45. h7 { mating } ) 
44. Rdd7 { threatening mate in three. Final remarks 1. The key to this game was the timely e4-e5 advance, which gave White all the benefits but none of the drawbacks of this move. 2. The disadvantage in placing a knight on a5 is that after e4-e5 it takes many moves to reach the d5-square. By the time this knight reached d5, Black’s camp was already far too weakened. } ) 

 1-0

